## §4 Findings

Ordered by build impact. Severity is against S1/S2 delivery, not against the corpus.

### F1 — `EvidenceRef.hash` is absent from every artifact · HIGH · A-recoverable
Not one L15 artifact in the corpus records a content hash for the evidence it cites. Reviews
cite test *names*, pass counts, and gate outputs. This is the highest-severity single field
loss because §3.3.3 and §6.1b r2 make the hash load-bearing: "evidence never leaves PAEOS;
re-verification requires going back to PAEOS's CAS". Without a hash there is nothing to go
back *to*. The fix is trivial and deterministic — the emitter hashes the artifact file at
seal time, and `kernel/cas.py` (built by B0.2, in this very corpus) already provides the
addressing. **Fix before S1 ingest, not after.**

### F2 — Trade-offs are reasoned in prose, never authored as typed axes · MEDIUM · partial
Three of four goals contain a genuine objective exchange with a defensible resolution, and in
all three `resolution` extracts cleanly while `axis`/`side_a`/`side_b` do not. §6.1b sources
`TradeOff` from "the L06 trade-off matrix" — **no L06 trade-off matrix exists as a file
anywhere in the repository**. Consequence: `tradeoff_precedents`, the *only* method S2
delivers, would be matching on model-inferred axes. Two fixes, both needed: (a) add an axis
line to the L06 template so future goals author it; (b) for history, accept model-inferred
axes as `candidate` with an explicit `inferred` provenance marker — never `canonical`,
per ×5.

### F3 — Scar does not round-trip losslessly; Ω1's claim is false as built · HIGH · A-lost for history
§4.3 states SAKG's `Scar` "mirrors PAEOS's scar-graph so scars round-trip losslessly (Ω1)".
It does not. Two independent defects:

1. **Schema mismatch.** The live `runtime/memory/__init__.py::Scar` is
   `(id, signature, lesson, detection, severity)`. §4.3 requires
   `(root_cause, detection_signature, guard_action)`. There is **no `root_cause` and no
   `guard_action`** in the runtime type. Even a perfect emitter reading the live scar-graph
   loses two of three required fields.
2. **No durable scar-graph.** `ScarStore` holds `self._scars: dict[str, Scar]` in process
   memory and is never persisted. Grep confirms no scar artifact on disk anywhere. For every
   historical goal the "scar-graph" §6.1b pulls from **does not exist**; scars must be
   reconstructed from prose, which is why `detection_signature` is the corpus's only
   A-lost field.

This is the finding that came closest to firing the NO-GO. It does not fire it because
`root_cause` and `guard_action` *are* recoverable from CER-4 Adversary-pass prose in the two
goals that had real defects (B0.2 scored 2.5/3), and because the runtime schema is a Zone-2
type that can be extended cheaply. But **Ω1's "losslessly" must be struck or qualified**, and
the runtime `Scar` type must gain the two fields before S3.

### F4 — The seal event has no machine-readable predicate · HIGH · A-recoverable
§6.1b's trigger is "PAEOS goal reaches L16 (Promotion) and seals". Nothing in the corpus
makes that state machine-detectable. `ledger/decisions/` **does not exist**. B0.2's seal is
the prose string "**Founder verdict (2026-07-27): B0.2 RATIFIED.**"; B2.G's is an *unticked*
markdown checkbox (`- [ ] Founder: ratify B2.G`). An emitter cannot reliably answer "is this
goal sealed?" and therefore cannot decide when to emit. Fix: L16 writes a structured
disposition record (the `ledger/decisions/` path G-S1-GO already assumes) with an explicit
status field.

### F5 — §6.1b's bundle omits types two ratified methods traverse · HIGH · spec-internal gap
The bundle emits 7 of the 12 §4.3 types. Missing: `Problem`, `Objective`, `Mechanism`,
`Pattern` (and `Outcome`, correctly deferred). Cross-referencing §5.2:

| Method (§5.2, fixed by PAEOS-9 §10.1) | Types it traverses | Servable from the S1 bundle? |
|---|---|---|
| `tradeoff_precedents` | TradeOff, **Objective**, Constraint, Decision, Outcome | **Partially** — TradeOff endpoints can only be Constraints |
| `pattern_retrieval` | **Problem**, Option, **Mechanism**, **Pattern**, Scar | **No** — entry node type is not emitted |
| `query_architectures` | Component, **Pattern**, Decision, Outcome | Partially |
| `dependency_impact` | Component, Outcome | Yes (Outcome via S3) |
| `memory_augment` | Scar + Similarity edges | No — Similarity edges are not emitted |

This bites S2 directly: `tradeoff_precedents` is S2's only method and its `TradeOff` nodes
cannot reach an `Objective`. `Objective` and `Problem` *are* extractable from PAEOS artifacts
at roughly the quality of `Constraint` — acceptance criteria and the CER-4 "(c) which parts
became stronger" answer encode them. The fix is to add both to the bundle in `F-BUNDLE`, and
it is cheap **only if made before the bundle contract is frozen**. This finding alone
justifies having run the probe before `F-ENVELOPE`/`F-BUNDLE`.

### F6 — The named L15 sources do not exist; the real sources are different files · MEDIUM
§6.1b names five producers. Actual state:

| §6.1b source | Reality in this repository |
|---|---|
| decision-graph (ADR records) | No per-goal ADRs. Constitutional decisions live in `proposals/PAEOS-IP-*.md`; goal decisions live in review prose. |
| L06 trade-off matrix | Does not exist (F2). |
| L04 candidates | No candidate-set artifact; rejected options survive incidentally in prose. |
| L11 implementation contract | Does not exist as a file; constraints come from the acceptance table. |
| L08 architecture artifact set | `docs/IMPLEMENTATION_ATLAS.md` — but it stops at B0.5 despite the L15 rule mandating a row per task. |
| scar-graph | In-memory only (F3). |

Extraction still works because the *review documents* are unexpectedly good substitutes. But
the S1 extractor must be written against `reviews/tasks/*.md`, `docs/*-constitutional-review.md`,
`ledger/debt/*.md`, and `proposals/*.md` — **not** against the artifact names in §6.1b. §6.1b
should be amended to name the real sources.

### F7 — §8's "round-trip is lossless" is ambiguous and currently unpassable on one reading · MEDIUM
S1's exit criterion reads "A sealed PAEOS goal produces a valid bundle; round-trip is
lossless." On the reading *prose → bundle*, this probe proves it can never pass: 0.16 loss is
irreducible against existing artifacts, and 4 fields are A-lost. On the reading *bundle →
storage → bundle*, it is achievable and is the right conformance test. **Recommend §8 be
clarified to the second reading**, with extraction yield measured separately against this
probe's metric and a stated floor. Without the clarification S1 has a criterion it cannot
satisfy, and the slice would fail for the wrong reason.

### F8 — Two systematic envelope partials, both cheap · LOW
`provenance.origin` carries a goal id but **no run id anywhere in the corpus** — emitters can
supply it going forward, history cannot. `provenance.author_kind` is genuinely undecidable
for bootstrap-era goals ("humans play all roles at bootstrap"); `unknown` is the honest value
and the enum should admit it. `summary` requires generation, not extraction — acceptable,
but the generated string must be written once and hashed into `corpus_hash`, or §5.1 r3
reproducibility is compromised.

